  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure will continue to move east across the area today.\nThis will allow for return flow and additional moisture to enter\nthe region. High temperatures will be warmer today in the 80s\nfor most with some isolated highs near 90. The combination of\ntemperatures approaching convective temperatures, additional\nmoisture with dew points in the 60s, and a lake breeze could\nallow for some showers to form in interior Northeast Ohio.\nCoverage will be fairly limited, but will maintain some low\nPoPs. For most, it will just be a dry, typical late July day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe main weather concern for the entire forecast period will be\na low pressure system that will move through the region starting\non Saturday and not fully exit until Monday evening. This system\ncontinues to trend slower with the 00z guidance and much of the\nforecast area may end up staying dry during the daytime hours \non Saturday with just some scattered activity in the western \nhalf of the forecast area. The bulk of the rain and storm \nactivity seems to be honing in on Saturday night into Sunday. \nThe environment with incoming low and upper trough moving \nthrough the region will be favorable for efficient rainfall with\nPW values over 2 inches in some model soundings in Northwest \nand North Central Ohio. Storms should be progressing across the \nregion, despite the sluggish low. However, there is enough \nforcing to get multiple storms to develop and there is concern \nfor training storms, which could pose a flood risk. Portions of\nthe western forecast area are in a Day 2 Slight Risk for \nexcessive rainfall to highlight the heavy rain and flash flood \npotential on Saturday night. Portions of NE OH and NW PA are in\na Day 3 Slight Risk for excessive rainfall as rain will continue\non Sunday for the eastern half of the area. The system will exit\nby Monday and have PoPs trending down Sunday evening and onward. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nFor next week, the upper trough that supported the system\nthrough the region this weekend will become elongated and then\nget pinched off from the main upper flow moving through eastern\nCanada. This will allow for a cut off upper low to form over \nthe central Appalachians. With this feature to the southeast of \nthe forecast area, tough to be highly confident in rain chances.\nHowever, this feature could provide just enough lift in a \ntypical summer atmosphere to generate some scattered showers \nand maybe some storm. The best chance for any rain would be \nduring the daytime hours with help from peak heating.",
